{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-16fecc5b-3e4b-4139-98aa-8996bdf0e869","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-97a88f0af59e9449","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9020-myskupfd/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-E001.md","content":"# AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm serving functional and performance parity\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic runs the validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct accelerators that establishes first-hand functional and performance parity data for vLLM ROCm serving of the supported model set, and produces the go/no-go scope that determines which models are productized. It resolves the strategy's central unknown — whether upstream vLLM ROCm builds deliver acceptable parity for our supported models — which the serving runtime productization work is gated on.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Secure access to dedicated AMD Instinct GPU nodes (via procurement or partner-lab access); the access grant itself is externally controlled and tracked as a risk, while the epic's deliverable is the validated parity report.\n- Stand up vLLM ROCm serving on the AMD Instinct nodes using upstream ROCm runtime builds, deployed through the platform serving stack (InferenceServices served by a vLLM ROCm runtime), so parity is measured on the path users will actually run.\n- For every model in the supported model set, run functional validation of vLLM serving on ROCm and record pass/fail against defined correctness criteria.\n- For every model in the supported model set, measure serving performance (throughput and latency) on AMD Instinct hardware and record a comparison against the CUDA baseline.\n- Compile the campaign report: per-model functional and performance results, environment details (driver, ROCm, runtime image versions), and the explicit go/no-go scope — the list of models cleared for productization.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A functional parity result (pass/fail against defined criteria) is recorded for every model in the supported model set.\n- Performance measurements (throughput and latency) are recorded for every model in the supported model set on AMD Instinct hardware, alongside a CUDA baseline comparison.\n- The serving path is exercised through the platform serving stack: InferenceServices deployed via KServe using a vLLM ROCm runtime on AMD GPU nodes, confirming the serving control plane requires no code changes for AMD accelerators.\n- The final report states the go/no-go scope: the explicit list of models cleared for productization, with the supporting evidence.\n- All results are reproducible from the recorded job configurations and environment versions (driver, ROCm, runtime image digests).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0)**: A validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Hardware access is unresolved (procurement or partner-lab); the grant timing is externally controlled and is the strategy's highest schedule risk. The acceptance criteria sit on the epic's deliverable (the validated report), not on the availability of hardware.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":35348,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-86125cd2e72d3245","agent_id":"ad4feccb015d8082a","session_id":"a1fb373b-b101-443e-9fd4-5e641a172da3","uuid":"1a0a8e82-4112-4b2a-92c7-a8df26018532","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:17:44.920Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9020"}
