{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-964bf55fbb235d6c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n3\ttitle: 'AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm serving functional and performance\n4\t  parity'\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n6\tcomponent: Serving Runtimes\n7\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n8\ttype: Investigation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tinvestigation_signals:\n12\t  question_specificity: 1\n13\t  source_accessibility: 0\n14\t  local_runnability: 0\n15\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -2\n16\t  human_judgment_required: -1\n17\timplementation_type: null\n18\tai_signals: null\n19\tai_implementability: Low\n20\tai_implementability_score: -2\n21\tjira_key: null\n22\tbranch: null\n23\tgated_by: null\n24\tgate_failure_impact: null\n25\t---\n26\t# AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm serving functional and performance parity\n27\t\n28\t## Description\n29\t\n30\tThis epic runs the validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct accelerators that establishes first-hand functional and performance parity data for vLLM ROCm serving of the supported model set, and produces the go/no-go scope that determines which models are productized. It resolves the strategy's central unknown — whether upstream vLLM ROCm builds deliver acceptable parity for our supported models — which the serving runtime productization work is gated on.\n31\t\n32\t## Scope\n33\t\n34\t- Secure access to dedicated AMD Instinct GPU nodes (via procurement or partner-lab access); the access grant itself is externally controlled and tracked as a risk, while the epic's deliverable is the validated parity report.\n35\t- Stand up vLLM ROCm serving on the AMD Instinct nodes using upstream ROCm runtime builds, deployed through the platform serving stack (InferenceServices served by a vLLM ROCm runtime), so parity is measured on the path users will actually run.\n36\t- For every model in the supported model set, run functional validation of vLLM serving on ROCm and record pass/fail against defined correctness criteria.\n37\t- For every model in the supported model set, measure serving performance (throughput and latency) on AMD Instinct hardware and record a comparison against the CUDA baseline.\n38\t- Compile the campaign report: per-model functional and performance results, environment details (driver, ROCm, runtime image versions), and the explicit go/no-go scope — the list of models cleared for productization.\n39\t\n40\t## Acceptance Criteria\n41\t\n42\t- A functional parity result (pass/fail against defined criteria) is recorded for every model in the supported model set.\n43\t- Performance measurements (throughput and latency) are recorded for every model in the supported model set on AMD Instinct hardware, alongside a CUDA baseline comparison.\n44\t- The serving path is exercised through the platform serving stack: InferenceServices deployed via KServe using a vLLM ROCm runtime on AMD GPU nodes, confirming the serving control plane requires no code changes for AMD accelerators.\n45\t- The final report states the go/no-go scope: the explicit list of models cleared for productization, with the supporting evidence.\n46\t- All results are reproducible from the recorded job configurations and environment versions (driver, ROCm, runtime image digests).\n47\t\n48\t## HLR Traceability\n49\t\n50\t- **HLR-1 (P0)**: A validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization.\n51\t\n52\t## Risks / Notes\n53\t\n54\t- Hardware access is unresolved (procurement or partner-lab); the grant timing is externally controlled and is the strategy's highest schedule risk. The acceptance criteria sit on the epic's deliverable (the validated report), not on the availability of hardware.\n55\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b1fc87e5b43481c","agent_id":"a42f09c51428f0558","session_id":"a1fb373b-b101-443e-9fd4-5e641a172da3","uuid":"dc519c8d-6e4c-4b05-aa9c-c729a8ca80c1","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:20:40.065Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-964bf55fbb235d6c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
