{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0a0374c1b1b7573","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020 (AMD GPU / ROCm enablement for serving and training)\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm serving functional and performance parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for campaign-cleared models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support for single- and multi-GPU PyTorchJobs | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling for training workload admission | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | AMD ROCm support documentation (hardware, drivers, validated models) | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n21\t\n22\t## Dependency DAG\n23\t\n24\t```mermaid\n25\tgraph TD\n26\t    E001[\"E001 Validation campaign (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization\"]\n27\t    E004[\"E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota\"] --> E003[\"E003 PyTorch ROCm training\"]\n28\t    E001 --> E005[\"E005 Documentation\"]\n29\t    E002 --> E005\n30\t    E003 --> E005\n31\t    E004 --> E005\n32\t```\n33\t\n34\t## DAG Justification\n35\t\n36\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n37\t|------|------|-----------|\n38\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's go/no-go outcome determines which models the productized runtime supports — a true gate (`rewrite`: scope narrows to cleared models; if no model clears, productization is removed). |\n39\t| E004 → E003 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | PyTorchJob training admission through Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota builds on the quota/admission capability; multi-GPU gang admission cannot be validated before quota handling exists. |\n40\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 + content input | Docs state the validated model list, which is the campaign's deliverable. |\n41\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs describe the productized runtime. |\n42\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs describe the training usage path. |\n43\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs describe amd.com/gpu quota usage. |\n44\t\n45\tNo edge between E001/E002 and E003/E004: serving and training streams are architecturally distinct (different components, different teams, no shared artifact). They share only the AMD hardware pool — a coordination risk recorded in Health Warnings, not a dependency. No `konflux-onboarding` epic is generated (see Component Mapping Notes).\n46\t\n47\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n48\t\n49\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n50\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n51\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware; produces go/no-go scope. |\n52\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | Productization of the vLLM ROCm serving runtime, scoped to cleared models. |\n53\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E003, E004 | Split along component boundary (Training Operator vs. Distributed Workloads), both owned by the Distributed Workloads team. |\n54\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 | Docs epic priority is P1 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority `Major` → P1), not P2. |\n55\t\n56\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No redundant/overlapping HLR pairs were found, and no lower-priority HLR is bundled into a higher-priority epic (HLR-3's two-epic split is a component boundary split, not a priority split).\n57\t\n58\t## Health Warnings\n59\t\n60\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements section. Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLR text; owner teams were inferred from `.context/architecture-context/` owning-team fields (RHOAI Serving for KServe/Serving Runtimes; Distributed Workloads for Training Operator/Distributed Workloads). Human verification of team assignments recommended.\n61\t- **Hardware availability (Open Question 1)** is an availability precondition externally controlled (procurement or partner-lab access), not a DAG node or acceptance criterion. The validation campaign epic includes *securing* access as part of its work, and its acceptance criteria sit on the deliverable (the validated parity report). If access stalls, the entire strategy slips — highest-schedule-risk item.\n62\t- **Upstream ROCm image cadence (Open Question 2)** is externally controlled timing. It does not change which epics exist, so it is recorded as a schedule risk on the runtime productization epic (with a pinned-upstream-release acceptance criterion), not as an Investigation or an edge.\n63\t- **KServe treated as passive**: KServe is listed as an affected component but no code changes are anticipated — InferenceService deployment on AMD GPU nodes is exercised as acceptance criteria on the campaign and productization epics. If campaign findings reveal KServe changes are needed, a KServe epic must be added.\n64\t- **E001 size watch item (re-split trigger)**: the validation campaign is one logical pass over the full supported model set on externally scheduled hardware and could exceed the ~2-week epic guideline. It is intentionally not split — a split would duplicate the campaign (shared hardware access, per-model job configurations, and a single parity report) and fragment the go/no-go deliverable that E002's scope depends on. Re-split trigger: if the campaign outgrows a sprint of effort, split it along model-family lines, or split performance benchmarking out of functional validation.\n65\t- **Documentation epic team**: the docs epic spans both teams' output (serving + training); it is assigned to RHOAI Serving (owner of the P0 stream) and must be technically reviewed by both teams.\n66\t\n67\t## Component Mapping Notes\n68\t\n69\t- All epic `component` values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`: `Serving Runtimes` (E001, E002), `Training Operator` (E003), `Distributed Workloads` (E004), `Documentation` (E005).\n70\t- **KServe**: passive — validated as acceptance criteria on E001/E002 (see Health Warnings).\n71\t- **No `konflux-onboarding` epic**: the midstream vLLM repo is already Konflux-onboarded per `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/serving-runtimes.md`. The ROCm image is a new build variant in an existing onboarded repo, so pipeline work folds into the productization epic, gated by a \"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion rather than a separate epic or DAG edge.\n72\t- All four strategy components exist in architecture context — no new-component onboarding chain (repo-onboarding / license-validation) is required. License scanning of the ROCm image's transitive dependencies is absorbed into the productization epic's release checklist rather than a separate epic, since the repo and its CUDA builds already ship through the same pipeline.\n73\t\n74\t## Tiered Delivery\n75\t\n76\t- **Tier 1 (independent value, starts immediately)**: E001 (risk-reduction validation producing the go/no-go data), E004 and E003 (training on ROCm delivers user value independent of the serving go/no-go outcome).\n77\t- **Tier 2 (gated)**: E002 — productization is gated by the campaign outcome (E001) and scoped to the models it clears.\n78\t- E005 (documentation) follows all implementation work per Rule 11.\n79\t\n80\t## Conditional Decomposition\n81\t\n82\tThe campaign's go/no-go outcome is a continuum (each model independently clears or fails), not a small set of distinct structural outcomes, so no branch files are generated. The gate is recorded on the productization epic as `gated_by` with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` (scope narrows to the cleared model list; if no model clears, the epic is removed and results feed back to the strategy).\n83\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa5ed1c6ebe45912","agent_id":"aba3e9d5a040feddf","session_id":"a1fb373b-b101-443e-9fd4-5e641a172da3","uuid":"9bfac969-aeee-44b0-8d9d-63ccfe79024c","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:24:01.625Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0a0374c1b1b7573","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9020"}
